## CP1: Seasonal Respiratory Vaccination Programme Optimisation

### Intervention 1A: Targeted Outreach Flu Vaccination for Under-Vaccinated Populations

**Problem addressed:** Inequitable vaccination uptake in deprived communities, ethnic minorities, people experiencing homelessness, and those with serious mental illness — groups with the highest respiratory disease burden.

**Mechanism:** Community outreach (mobile units, faith settings, pharmacies, workplace) removes structural access barriers; cultural and linguistic adaptation addresses hesitancy; opportunistic vaccination at every NHS contact point reduces missed opportunities.

**Evidence:** Strong. Cochrane systematic review (Jacobson Vann et al., 2018) demonstrates patient reminder and recall systems consistently improve immunisation rates. UKHSA real-world effectiveness monitoring confirms seasonal hospitalisation reduction of 30–60% in vaccine-matched seasons (Jefferson et al., multiple Cochrane updates). PHE/UKHSA community outreach programme evaluations confirm feasibility in ethnic minority and deprived populations (UKHSA, 2021–22 COVID vaccination equity reports, applicable by mechanism transfer).

**Evidence classification:** Strong (Cochrane level for vaccination; strong observational for outreach modality)

**Implementation level:** Organisational / Policy

**Feasibility:** High within 1–3 years. Community pharmacy infrastructure already exists. Incremental cost per additional vaccination is low.

**Equity note:** Directly addresses documented uptake inequalities. Uptake rates must be monitored and reported disaggregated by IMD quintile, ethnicity, and housing status. *Risk of worsening inequalities if digital-only recall systems are used without analogue fallback.*

**Metrics:** Flu vaccination coverage by deprivation quintile and ethnicity; influenza-related A&E attendances per winter season; healthcare worker influenza sickness absence rates.

---

### Intervention 1B: RSV Vaccination Programme for Adults ≥75 and Pregnant Women

**Problem addressed:** RSV is an underrecognised cause of winter hospitalisation in older adults comparable in burden to influenza, historically without a licensed vaccine.

**Mechanism:** Arexvy and Abrysvo vaccines reduce RSV-associated lower respiratory tract disease by approximately 67–89% in adults ≥60. Integration into existing autumn vaccination infrastructure maximises efficiency.

**Evidence:** Strong for vaccine efficacy. Phase 3 RCT evidence (Papi et al., 2023, *NEJM*; Walsh et al., 2023, *NEJM*). JCVI UK recommendation (2023); NHS England RSV programme launched 2024. Real-world NHS programme effectiveness evidence is in early stages — flagged as emerging.

**Evidence classification:** Strong for clinical efficacy; emerging for NHS programme-level impact.

**Implementation level:** Policy / Clinical

**Feasibility:** High within 1–3 years. Programme already initiated.

**Equity note:** Requires active monitoring for uptake gaps mirroring historical flu vaccination inequalities in ethnic minority and deprived communities.

**Metrics:** RSV vaccination uptake in ≥75 cohort; RSV-coded winter hospital admissions; excess winter mortality (respiratory cause).

---

### Intervention 1C: Healthcare Worker Influenza and Respiratory Vaccination

**Problem addressed:** HCW respiratory illness reduces frontline clinical capacity during winter peak and contributes to nosocomial transmission to vulnerable patients.

**Mechanism:** Occupational health-delivered vaccination with opt-out invitation, flexible access (all shifts), and senior leadership visible endorsement.

**Evidence:** Strong. Cochrane review (Thomas et al., 2016) confirms HCW vaccination associated with reduced patient mortality in care home settings. UKHSA annual HCW vaccine effectiveness monitoring supports programme.

**Evidence classification:** Strong

**Implementation level:** Organisational

**Feasibility:** Very high. Infrastructure already exists. Requires annual quality improvement.

**Equity note:** BAME HCW staff report higher vaccine hesitancy in some settings; culturally competent communication from trusted peers is more effective than mandated approaches.

**Metrics:** HCW flu vaccination uptake by staff group and trust; HCW respiratory sickness absence rate October–March.

---

## CP2: Respiratory Chronic Disease Management (COPD/Asthma)

### Intervention 2A: Pre-Winter Structured COPD Annual Review with Personalised Action Plans and Rescue Medication

**Problem addressed:** Predictable COPD exacerbations drive a disproportionate share of winter emergency admissions. Many are preventable through early self-initiated treatment.

**Mechanism:** Systematic annual review (ideally September–October) incorporating spirometry confirmation, MRC dyspnoea score, exacerbation history, inhaler technique, vaccination status, and provision of a written personalised action plan including rescue antibiotics and prednisolone. Patients initiate treatment at first exacerbation symptom onset, reducing severity and preventing hospital-level escalation.

**Evidence:** Strong. NICE NG115 (2019, updated 2022). Effing et al. (2016) Cochrane review confirms self-management interventions with action plans reduce respiratory-related hospitalisations (RR 0.70, 95% CI 0.56–0.88). Lenferink et al. (2017) Cochrane review. BTS/SIGN guidelines.

**Evidence classification:** Strong

**Implementation level:** Clinical / Organisational

**Feasibility:** High within 1–3 years through PCN QOF infrastructure. Timing to pre-winter requires practice-level workflow change.

**Equity note:** Health literacy barriers require plain-language and multilingual action plan materials. Rescue packs require patient education to prevent inappropriate antibiotic use. *Antibiotic stewardship tension requires explicit patient selection criteria.*

**Metrics:** Proportion of COPD patients with annual review completed before November; rescue pack prescription rate; COPD emergency admissions per 1,000 registered COPD patients (NHS RightCare benchmark).

---

### Intervention 2B: Pulmonary Rehabilitation (PR) Access Expansion with Post-Exacerbation Referral

**Problem addressed:** PR is one of the most cost-effective NHS interventions for COPD with Cochrane-level evidence, yet fewer than 15% of eligible patients access it nationally.

**Mechanism:** Supervised exercise and education programme delivered within 4 weeks of COPD exacerbation reduces readmission rates by approximately 50% and improves exercise capacity and quality of life. Systematic post-discharge referral and telerehabilitation for housebound patients extends reach.

**Evidence:** Very strong. McCarthy et al. (2015) Cochrane review (65 RCTs). Puhan et al. (2016) Cochrane review confirms 90-day admission reduction. NICE NG115 Grade A recommendation. NHSE GIRFT respiratory report identifies PR referral gap as major unwarranted variation.

**Evidence classification:** Strong

**Implementation level:** Clinical / Organisational

**Feasibility:** Medium. Workforce capacity is the binding constraint; physiotherapy shortage is documented nationally. Within 3–5 years for systematic coverage. Digital/hybrid PR as interim pathway within 1–3 years.

**Equity note:** Transport and access barriers disproportionately affect rural and deprived populations. Home-based or telerehabilitation models are a necessary equity safeguard, not an inferior alternative.

**Flag — undervalued and underdelivered:** This is among the most robustly evidenced and consistently underprovided NICE-recommended interventions in England. The gap between evidence quality and implementation fidelity is exceptional and represents a priority for commissioning attention.

**Metrics:** PR referral rate per 1,000 COPD admissions; PR completion rate; 90-day COPD readmission rate; NHSE GIRFT PR gap indicator.

---

### Intervention 2C: Inhaler Technique Optimisation and Low-Carbon Prescribing

**Problem addressed:** 40–90% of patients use inhalers incorrectly, reducing drug delivery and precipitating exacerbations. High-GWP metered-dose inhalers contribute disproportionately to NHS carbon footprint.

**Mechanism:** Structured inhaler technique assessment at every respiratory review using standardised tools. Prescribing transition to dry powder inhalers (DPIs) or next-generation low-GWP devices where clinically appropriate improves both outcomes and sustainability.

**Evidence:** Strong for technique errors (Sanchis et al., 2016, *Chest*). NHS Greener NHS programme inhaler carbon data published. NICE NG115 and NG80 mandate technique assessment.

**Evidence classification:** Strong (clinical); emerging (sustainability co-benefits at system level)

**Implementation level:** Clinical

**Feasibility:** High within 1–3 years. Low cost. Integrates into existing clinical encounters.

**Equity note:** Device-switching without adequate patient counselling and training risks worsening control in patients with poor inhaler technique. Equity safeguards required for DPI transition programmes. *Risk of unintentionally worsening outcomes if implementation is financially rather than clinically driven.*

**Metrics:** Proportion of COPD/asthma patients with documented technique assessment; DPI:MDI prescribing ratio; carbon emissions per 1,000 respiratory prescriptions (kg CO2e).

